[module-registry name=cx-modules
  [doc [#
THE MODULE REGISTRY — one row per shipped module surface, and the single
place its RING is DECLARED (RULED: 1427-f, OL-15).

Before this file the placement of a module was stated in four places by
implication — the spec's directory, the corpus's `ring=` header, the
directory its V code sits in (`vcx/code` = Ring 1, `vcx/platform` = Ring 2),
and its `[?lib]` namespace — and nothing compared them. #1077 and OL-14
record what that costs. This file is the declaration; `make placement-gate`
(scripts/placement_gate.cx) refuses a tree where any of the four disagrees
with the row, a spec under stdlib/ or platform/ with no row, or a row with
no corpus.

Derived FROM this file: the catalog step (scripts/stdlib_catalog_gate.cx),
the two bundled-surface counts asserted in vcx/tests/stdlib_umbrella_test.v,
the namespace migration tool (`cx --migrate-namespace registry/modules.cxd`)
and the two std-lib READMEs.

COLUMNS
  name    the module's call-site prefix — `[?lib '<ns>/<name>']` binds it
  ring    1 = pure/local (Ring 1), 2 = serves or reaches a store/protocol
          (Ring 2), 0 = a Ring-0 surface synthesized by the codec registry
  ns      the resolver namespace: cx-stdlib (Ring 1), cx-platform (Ring 2),
          cx-xap, cx-fabric, cx-x. `none` = no `[?lib]` surface (natives,
          backend engines, design texts)
  kind    module   a `[?lib]`-resolvable module
          natives  a dispatch family with no module surface
          backend  an external engine reached through core verbs
          design   an approved design text with no surface of its own
          codec    a surface synthesized by the codec registry (codec.md §3)
  spec    the normative text — its directory must match the ring
  corpus  the graded fixtures — its directory must match the ring
  source  the bundled CX source (stdlib/*.cx), or `none`
  code    the V file(s) carrying the dispatch — every one under the ring's
          own directory
  half    a DECLARED Ring-1 half of a Ring-2 module (1427-d): the pure codec
          or client a Ring-1 consumer may need for profile composition. It
          stays in vcx/code and is named HERE, so the placement check reads
          it as declared rather than as a ring violation. The split trigger
          is the first Ring-1 CONSUMER of a half — that is how `http-client`
          became its own Ring-1 module (1427-e)
  status  the module-meta status the spec carries

MEMBERSHIP TEST (spec/03-approved/core/cx_partition.md §10, carried from
ledger/partition_I3_ring12_split.md): a module that imports the store or a
protocol module, or that SERVES, is Ring 2; otherwise it is Ring 1. A module
lives in the ring of its HIGHEST verb; a pure half a Ring-1 consumer needs
becomes its own Ring-1 module.
#]]

